"""Additional original comprehension, reasoning and static-awareness material."""
def extend(topic, add):
    topic('algebra','Quant','Linear equations & quadratic factorisation','Preserve equality by applying the same operation to both sides; x²−(a+b)x+ab=(x−a)(x−b).',
          'Define unknowns before translating a word problem. Check every root against the original conditions.',
          'Look for two factors with the required sum and product.','A root of a transformed equation can violate the original domain.')
    topic('word-classes','Verbal','Parts of speech, plurals & one-word substitutions','Identify a word’s function in its sentence; distinguish a general category from a precise term.',
          'Adverbs modify actions or descriptions; adjectives describe nouns. Some borrowed nouns have irregular plurals.',
          'Use sentence function and meaning together.','A broad term can be related without being the most precise answer.')
    topic('web-foundations','GeneralAwareness','Web, APIs & version control','HTTP methods express request semantics; status codes express outcomes; Git records content history.',
          'PUT and PATCH have different update semantics. CSS controls presentation, while APIs define software interfaces.',
          'Learn method–purpose and status-code–meaning pairs.','Do not confuse creating a Git branch with switching the checkout.')
    topic('programming-semantics','Coding','Types, coercion & language semantics','Evaluate operators under the named language’s rules; a const binding is not a deeply immutable object.',
          'Distinguish strings from numbers, reference types from primitives and invocation receivers from function ownership.',
          'Write down the type of each intermediate value.','JavaScript loose and strict equality perform different checks.')
    static=[
      ('Which cell structure contains most of a human cell’s genetic material?','Nucleus',['Ribosome','Cell wall','Lysosome'],'Most human cellular DNA is stored in the nucleus; mitochondria contain a smaller additional genome.'),
      ('Which tissue carries water from roots to other parts of most vascular plants?','Xylem',['Phloem','Epidermis only','Pollen'],'Xylem transports water and dissolved minerals; phloem mainly transports organic nutrients.'),
      ('Which blood cells chiefly transport oxygen using haemoglobin?','Red blood cells',['Platelets','White blood cells','Stem cells only'],'Red blood cells contain haemoglobin, which binds and transports oxygen.'),
      ('Which chemical element has the symbol Fe?','Iron',['Fluorine','Francium','Fermium'],'Fe is the chemical symbol for iron, derived from ferrum.'),
      ('What is the SI unit of energy?','joule',['watt','newton','ampere'],'A joule is an energy unit; a watt measures energy transferred per unit time.'),
      ('Which lens is thicker at its centre than at its edges?','Convex lens',['Concave lens','Plane mirror','Concave mirror'],'A convex lens is thicker centrally and can converge parallel light rays in air.'),
      ('Sound normally cannot propagate through which medium?','A vacuum',['Air','Water','Steel'],'Sound is a mechanical wave that requires material particles; a vacuum provides none.'),
      ('Which atmospheric layer contains most of Earth’s protective ozone layer?','Stratosphere',['Troposphere','Mesosphere','Exosphere'],'Most atmospheric ozone is concentrated in the stratosphere, where it absorbs much harmful ultraviolet radiation.'),
      ('At standard atmospheric pressure, pure water freezes at which Celsius temperature?','0°C',['100°C','−100°C','32°C'],'The ordinary freezing point of pure water at standard pressure is zero degrees Celsius.'),
      ('Which part of a plant is the principal site of photosynthesis in most familiar land plants?','Leaves',['Roots','Seeds','Petals only'],'Leaves contain much of the plant’s chlorophyll-bearing tissue and are its principal photosynthetic organs.'),
      ('Which process changes water vapour into liquid droplets?','Condensation',['Evaporation','Melting','Sublimation'],'Condensation is the gas-to-liquid transition, often producing droplets when humid air cools.'),
      ('Which instrument measures atmospheric pressure?','Barometer',['Ammeter','Voltmeter','Hydrometer'],'A barometer measures atmospheric pressure; the other instruments measure current, potential difference and liquid density.')]
    for q,a,d,e in static:
        add('science',q,a,e,d)
    passages=[
      ('A college library moved from a first-come waiting list to a booking system. During a six-week pilot, average waiting time fell from eighteen minutes to eleven. The number of daily visitors remained almost unchanged. Staff also added a temporary evening counter during the pilot. The librarian recommended continuing the pilot after the temporary counter closed before deciding whether the booking system alone justified a permanent investment.',[
       ('What most directly limits a causal claim about the booking system?','The temporary counter changed service capacity at the same time.',['Daily visitors rose sharply.','No waiting time was measured.','The booking system was removed.'],'The counter is a simultaneous intervention, so the decrease cannot be attributed solely to booking.'),
       ('Which proposal best tests the librarian’s stated concern?','Measure waiting time after the temporary counter closes while retaining booking.',['Count the number of books only.','Remove all records of waiting time.','Compare the library’s building height.'],'Keeping booking while removing the counter helps distinguish their contributions, although other controls can still matter.'),
       ('Which conclusion is supported by the reported figures?','Waiting time fell during the pilot, but booking alone has not been established as the cause.',['Booking must work equally well at every library.','Visitor numbers doubled.','The counter had no effect.'],'The observed reduction is established; the cause remains uncertain because two things changed together.')]),
      ('An electronics workshop compared two suppliers. Supplier A charged less per component, while Supplier B promised shorter delivery times. The workshop’s earlier shortages had interrupted assembly, but its records did not show how many interruptions were caused by late deliveries rather than inaccurate demand estimates. The purchasing team proposed recording the cause of each shortage for a month before deciding whether the higher price of B would reduce the workshop’s total cost.',[
       ('Why might the cheaper component price fail to minimise total cost?','Supply interruptions can impose costs beyond the component price.',['Component price never affects cost.','Supplier B is necessarily cheaper per component.','The workshop has no assembly process.'],'A lower purchase price can be offset by interruption costs; the passage does not establish the size of those costs.'),
       ('Which information does the team specifically lack?','How much of the shortage problem comes from delivery delays.',['The existence of two suppliers.','Whether A has a lower unit price.','Whether assembly has ever stopped.'],'The records do not distinguish late delivery from demand-estimation errors.'),
       ('What assumption would be needed to conclude that B fixes every shortage?','Every relevant shortage is caused by a delivery delay that B would prevent.',['Demand estimation can be inaccurate.','A charges a lower component price.','Assembly requires components.'],'That strong conclusion would require removing other causes as well as guaranteeing B prevents the delivery-related ones.')]),
      ('A town introduced refill stations to reduce disposable bottles. The first survey found that station users bought fewer bottles than non-users. However, the survey did not ask how often either group bought bottles before the stations opened. The report recommended comparing each group’s purchases before and after the introduction. It also noted that people already committed to reducing waste may have been more likely to use the stations.',[
       ('Which alternative explanation is explicitly suggested?','People who already avoided disposable bottles may have selected themselves into station use.',['The stations dispensed no water.','Non-users had never bought bottles.','The survey measured purchases before introduction.'],'Self-selection can explain a group difference without showing the station caused it.'),
       ('What additional measurement does the report recommend?','Before-and-after bottle purchases for both groups.',['Only the colour of each station.','Only the town’s population ten years earlier.','Only the age of station equipment.'],'The recommendation seeks changes over time within each group rather than a single cross-sectional difference.'),
       ('Which statement goes beyond the evidence?','The stations caused the entire difference in bottle purchasing.',['Station users reported fewer purchases.','Prior buying habits were not measured.','Self-selection is possible.'],'The observed group difference is not enough to establish that the intervention caused the entire gap.')]),
      ('A software team shortened its release checklist after finding that several steps repeated the same manual inspection. The new process kept automated tests and required a separate reviewer for changes to payment calculations. Routine documentation edits followed a lighter path. The team’s aim was to remove duplicate work while keeping stronger checks on changes with greater consequences. It planned to review incident reports monthly and restore a check if its removal introduced a recurring problem.',[
       ('What principle best describes the new review process?','Apply review effort according to a change’s consequences.',['Use no tests for any change.','Treat all changes as equally risky.','Avoid learning from incidents.'],'Payment changes receive stronger review while low-impact documentation edits use a lighter path.'),
       ('Which safeguard is explicitly retained for payment changes?','A separate reviewer.',['A ban on automated tests.','No human review.','Automatic approval of every calculation.'],'The passage specifically requires a separate reviewer for payment-calculation changes.'),
       ('Which inference about removed checks is justified?','Their removal is revisable if recurring incidents show a need.',['They can never be restored.','Every removed check was useless in all circumstances.','The team will ignore future incidents.'],'Monthly incident review can trigger restoration; the policy is conditional rather than irreversible.')]),
      ('A training group found that learners who completed daily quizzes performed better on its final assessment. The coordinator cautioned that quiz completion was voluntary: more motivated learners might both take quizzes and study longer. For the next course, the group proposed randomly assigning reminders to some learners while keeping all learning materials available to everyone. It would compare quiz participation and outcomes before deciding whether reminders improved learning.',[
       ('Why does the first observation not prove a quiz effect?','Motivation could influence both quiz completion and assessment performance.',['No one completed quizzes.','Every learner studied for exactly the same time.','Assessment results were never compared.'],'Motivation is a potential confounding factor named in the passage.'),
       ('What is randomly assigned in the proposed course?','Reminders',['Access to all learning materials.','Final assessment marks.','Learners’ motivation.'],'The proposal varies reminders, while materials remain available to all.'),
       ('Which causal question does the proposed design most directly address?','Whether assigning reminders changes participation and learning outcomes.',['Whether all quizzes everywhere are effective.','Whether motivation can never matter.','Whether the previous course had no motivated learners.'],'Randomising reminders tests the effect of the reminder assignment, not every possible claim about quizzes.')]),
      ('A delivery cooperative considered replacing some van trips with cargo bicycles. Bicycles had lower running costs and could use narrow streets, but their load capacity was smaller. A trial therefore assigned small parcels on dense routes to bicycles and retained vans for heavier loads. The report warned against comparing average delivery times without adjusting for route and parcel differences, because the vehicles had not been assigned comparable work.',[
       ('Why is a raw comparison of average delivery times potentially misleading?','The vehicles handled different routes and parcel loads.',['Both vehicle types always carried identical loads.','Bicycles had greater capacity than vans.','The report did not mention deliveries.'],'Differences in the assigned work can influence time independently of vehicle choice.'),
       ('Which work was assigned to bicycles in the trial?','Small parcels on dense routes.',['All heavy loads.','Every route without exception.','Only journeys outside the town.'],'That allocation is explicitly stated, while vans retained heavier work.'),
       ('Which claim is supported without adding assumptions?','Vehicle suitability depends partly on load and route characteristics.',['Bicycles are faster on every route.','Vans are unnecessary for any load.','Running cost is the only relevant factor.'],'The trial’s allocation and capacity discussion support context-dependent suitability, not a universal winner.')])]
    for passage,items in passages:
        for q,a,d,e in items:
            add('reading','Read the passage:\n'+passage+'\n\n'+q,a,e,d,difficulty='Hard' if 'causal' in q or 'assumption' in q else 'Medium')
    items=[
      ('Five distinct projects A,B,C,D,E occupy five consecutive slots. A must precede C, C must precede E, and B must precede D. How many schedules satisfy all constraints?',10,'Choose the three slots for the fixed chain A,C,E in C(5,3)=10 ways. The two remaining slots must be B,D in that order, giving one completion per choice.'),
      ('Exactly one of P,Q,R won a prize. P says “Q won”; Q says “R won”; R says “Q did not win”. Exactly one statement is true. Which winner possibilities remain?','P or Q','If P won, only R’s statement is true. If Q won, only P’s statement is true. If R won, both Q and R speak truth, contradicting exactly one.'),
      ('In a row of four people A,B,C,D facing north, A is immediately left of B; C is at the far right; D is not at the far left. Who occupies the second position from the left?','B','Since C is fourth, A,B could occupy1,2 or2,3. The latter leaves D first, which is forbidden. Hence the order is A,B,D,C.'),
      ('A route goes 8 km east, 6 km north and then 8 km west. What is the final displacement from the start?','6 km north','The east/west movements cancel exactly, leaving only the6 km northern component.'),
      ('All selected files are backed up. No backed-up file is temporary. A file is temporary. What must follow?','The file is not selected.','Selected implies backed up, and backed up excludes temporary. Therefore a temporary file cannot be selected.'),
      ('Four distinct tasks A,B,C,D have durations1,2,3,4 hours respectively. A and B must finish before C starts. D is independent. With two workers and no interruptions, what is the shortest total completion time?',5,'The total work is10 worker-hours, giving a lower bound of5 hours with two workers. Worker1 does B(0–2) then C(2–5); worker2 does A(0–1) then D(1–5), achieving that bound.')]
    wrong=[['8','12','20'],['Q only','R only','All P,Q,R'],['A','C','D'],['22 km east','8 km west','6 km south'],['The file is selected.','The file is backed up.','Every file is temporary.'],['4','6','7']]
    for i,(q,a,e) in enumerate(items):
        add(['arrangements','conclusion','seating','direction','syllogism','constraint-puzzles'][i],q,a,e,wrong[i],difficulty='Hard' if i in (0,1,5) else 'Medium')
